{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193103": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Endowed Director - Maguire Center for Ethics and Pubic Responsibility",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University Location: Dallas Southern Methodist University invites applications and nominations for the William F. May Endowed Director of the Cary M. Maguire Center for Ethics and Public Responsibility. The position is named in honor of Professor William F. May, founding director (1995–1998), distinguished ethicist and theologian, and first occupant of the SMU Maguire Chair in Ethics. The position carries both the prestige of SMU's endowed chair system and the institutional platform to shape ethical discourse and matters of public responsibility at a major research university in one of the nation's fastest-growing metropolitan regions , the fourth largest metropolitan area in the US . This is a rare opportunity for a scholar-leader of national or international distinction to hold simultaneous roles as endowed center director and tenured faculty member, shaping both the intellectual direction of a well-resourced center for ethics and public responsibility and teaching within an academic department commensurate with the candidate's disciplinary expertise. The Maguire Center also has a close working relationship with the Bush Presidential Library and Institute , which is located on the SMU Campus, offering outstanding opportunities for collaborative research and interaction with world leaders. The Director provides vision, scholarly leadership, operational oversight, and external representation for the Center. The position combines the responsibilities of an academic administrator with those of an active scholar, teacher, and public intellectual. The Director reports to the Office of the Provost and works in close partnership with the Ethics Advisory Board, the Faculty Advisory Committee, and university leadership across SMU's schools and colleges. The day -to- day functions of the Center are managed by the Center’s Associate Director. Required Ph.D or commensurate graduate degree in ethics, philosophy, political science, communication, law, theology, business, public policy, engineering, health, or a related field A distinguished scholarly record, or a promising research agend a in ethics that would be appropriate for appointment as a tenured professor at a R1 university Demonstrated commitment to the discipline of ethics and to connecting rigorous scholarship with public and civic life Proven , successful administrative or leadership experience with the ability to collaborate across academic disciplines Experience working with diverse, interdisciplinary groups toward collaborative solutions to complex ethical issues Preferred National and/or international visibility as a scholar, public intellectual, media commentator, or policy voice Established networks in academic ethics, civic leadership, philanthropy, or relevant professional communities Experience directing or significantly leading a center, institute, or comparable academic unit Track record of successful involv e ment in fundraising, grant acquisition, or donor stewardship Key Responsibilities Strategic Leadership & Vision: Set and advance a scholarly and programmatic vision through a multi-year strategic plan Academic & Intellectual Leadership: Maintain an active scholarly and teaching agenda, while providing intellectual leadership that connects faculty and student research External Relations, Community Engagement, and Resource Development: Serve as the Center’s public representative regionally, nationally, and internationally. SMU invites applications and nominations for this position. The search committee will begin reviewing applications Nov. 1 and will continue until the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. A complete application includes: A cover letter describing scholarly background, leadership philosophy, vision for the Center and how the applicant fits the position’s qualifications. A current curriculum vitae A statement of research and how it connects to the mission of the Center (2–3 pages) Names and contact information for three references (references will not be contacted without the applicant's prior knowledge) Applications should be submitted to: https://apply.interfolio.com/193103 For questions about the application or to nominate a candidate, email search committee chair Dr. D. Stephen Long at sdlong@smu.edu SMU is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191511": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #50160 The Reverend Charles R. Millikan, D.Min. Endowed Chair in Spiritual Care",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Perkins School of Theology Location: Dallas Perkins School of Theology at SMU is seeking to fill a full-time, faculty appointment to the newly established and endowed Charles R. Millikan Endowed Chair in Spiritual Care. Position to begin August 1, 2027. This is an open rank search for a full-time tenure-track or tenured appointment. The responsibilities and qualifications are as follows: Responsibilities A. Full-time teaching and scholarship in Spiritual Care and Chaplaincy Studies. 1. Teaching courses in the various degree programs of Perkins School of Theology and, if appropriate, in  the Ph.D. curricula of the Graduate Program in Religious Studies of Southern Methodist University. 2. Scholarship, including publications, in the ﬁeld of Spiritual Care and Chaplaincy Studies. 3. Leading curricula and programming in chaplaincy studies 4. Networking with hospitals, Clinical Pastoral Education (CPE) programs, and other ministry partners. B. Participation in the life and work of Perkins School of Theology, the Graduate Program in Religious Studies, and other parts of Southern Methodist University, including the structures of governance, exercising the responsibilities normally assumed by faculty members. A. Essential 1. Ph.D. or equivalent degree. 2. Demonstrated achievement or promise as a scholar and teacher in the ﬁelds of spiritual care, pastoral care, chaplaincy studies, and other cognate fields such as spirituality studies, trauma studies, inter-religious dialogue, organizational leadership, and/or theology. 3. Commitment to the primary mission of Perkins School of Theology to prepare persons for religious leadership in the church, chaplaincy settings (including healthcare systems), social agencies and other places of ministry, and for leadership in the academy. 4. Commitment to teaching in all the degree programs of Perkins School of Theology and in all approved modes of instruction (including in-person, online, and hybrid). 5. Commitment to working in a religiously and theologically varied community, especially in working with chaplaincy students from diverse religious and spiritual paths. B. Preferred 1. Ordination or membership in a Christian communion. 2. CPE training and certification. Applications must include: 1) a cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) statement of teaching philosophy, 4) an outline for a course syllabus in spiritual care, 5) statement of research agenda, 6) a writing or publication sample, and 7) three conﬁdential letters of recommendation. Submit application materials electronically to Interfolio https://apply.interfolio.com/191511, to the attention of Ms. Avery Pryer, Executive Assistant to the Dean. To receive priority consideration, complete applications must be received by October 15, 2026, and to ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by December 15, 2026. The committee will, however, continue to accept and review applications until the position is ﬁlled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is ﬁlled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191886": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #52061 Assistant Professor of Theology",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Perkins School of Theology Location: Dallas Perkins School of Theology at Southern Methodist University is seeking to fill a full-time, tenure-track faculty appointment in theology. Position to begin on August 1, 2027. The responsibilities and qualifications are as follows: I. Responsibilities A. Full-time teaching and scholarship in theology. 1. Teaching courses in the various degree programs of Perkins School of Theology and in the M.A. and Ph.D. curricula of the Graduate Program in Religious Studies of Southern Methodist University. Required courses in systematic theology are expected as well as electives in one’s areas of specialization and interest. Co-teaching may be required in systematic theology. 2. Scholarship, including publications, in the field of appointment. B. Participation in the life and work of Perkins School of Theology, the Graduate Program in Religious Studies, and other parts of Southern Methodist University, including the structures of governance, exercising the responsibilities normally assumed by faculty members. A. Essential: 1. Ph.D. or equivalent degree. 2. Demonstrated promise as a scholar and teacher in the field of theology. 3. Specialization in one or more of areas of theology, such as systematic theology, historical theology, philosophical theology, constructive theology, theology and culture, or contextual theology. 4. Commitment to the primary objectives both of Perkins School of Theology to prepare persons for leadership in the church and in Christian ministry, and of the Graduate Program in Religious Studies to prepare persons for leadership in the academy. 5. Commitment to teaching in all the degree programs of Perkins School of Theology and in all approved modes of instruction (including in-person, online, and hybrid). 6. Familiarity with the historic and ecumenical traditions of Christianity, including their Wesleyan forms. 7. Commitment to working in a religiously and theologically diverse and pluralistic community. 8. Knowledge of and commitment to the life of a faith community. B. Preferred: 1. Background, interests, perspectives, and abilities that complement those of other members of the faculty, with an openness to creative pedagogical and academic collaboration across disciplines. 2. Ability to teach Introduction to the History of Christianity. 3. Ordination or membership in a Methodist or other Christian communion. 4. Ability to engage in interdisciplinary research and teaching in the School of Theology and the wider University. 5. Certification or experience in online teaching. Applications must include: 1) cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) writing sample, 4) statement of teaching philosophy, 5) an outline for a course syllabus in theology, 6) statement of research agenda, and 7) three confidential letters of recommendation. Submit application materials electronically to: (Interfolio link). Submit application materials electronically to Interfolio https://apply.interfolio.com/191886, to the attention of Ms. Avery Pryer, Executive Assistant to the Dean. To receive priority consideration, complete applications must be received by October 15, 2026, and to ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by December 15, 2026. The committee will, however, continue to accept and review applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/143662": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Adjunct Professor of Applied Physiology and Sport Management",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Dallas, TX  Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Simmons School of Education and Human Development at Southern Methodist University in Dallas, Texas. As part of our faculty selection process, we set course schedules and make staffing decisions before the start of each semester. Consequently, we review adjunct faculty applications during June-July for the fall semester and November-December for the spring semester. Qualified candidates should be able to teach one or more of the following subjects: Cultural Intelligence in Sport and Health Strength and Conditioning Exercise Program Design Coaching and Leadership Technology in Sport Nutrition Applied Exercise Physiology Functional Biomechanics Nutrition Anatomy and Physiology Labs Exercise Physiology Labs Sport Management. Please review our Applied Physiology and Sport Management course offerings and submit a brief cover letter indicating your teaching interests, areas of expertise, and any previous undergraduate or graduate teaching experience. Your cover letter and CV will be kept on file for department chairs to review when staffing courses. Required Qualifications Masters degree in Exercise Science, Kinesiology, Coaching, Sport Management, or a related field. Preferred Qualifications Ph.D. in Exercise Science or related field or other terminal degree in allied health (i.e. DPT, PA). Upload your curriculum vitae, a list of classes previously taught (if any), and cover letter indicating your teaching interests."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/146398": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Teacher Preparation Program Supervisor (Houston)",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Houston, Texas The SMU Teacher Preparation Program is hiring experienced teachers and administrators to mentor the next generation of teachers in Houston. The Supervisor role largely requires one to: Complete online new supervisor training. (Expect information via email in early August/December.) Conduct an orientation for the cooperating teacher before the clinical teaching semester begins. This session should provide information concerning the program and required responsibilities. Conduct a clinical teacher orientation by the first day of the clinical teaching semester. Primarily, this session should review the Handbook. Monitor clinical teacher's notebook during the clinical teaching semester. Observe, assess, and evaluate the clinical teacher regarding teaching, planning, management, and professionalism (3 formal 45-minute observations per semester). Conduct mid-point and summative 3-way conference with the clinical teacher and cooperating teacher. Write recommendation letters. Act as a liaison between the school district and Teacher Preparation Program. A qualified candidate for this position meets the following criteria: 3 years teaching experience Master's degree Current Texas teaching certification and/or principal (mid-management) or superintendent certification Accomplished educator (as shown by student learning) Experience mentoring teachers Commitment for one academic year It is not required that the supervisor is a current district employee. We are reviewing applicants on an as needed basis. Please submit the following documents: Resume Cover letter that addresses all qualification criteria. Valid and active copy of Educator Certification."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/150922": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Teacher Preparation Program Supervisor (San Antonio)",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: San Antonio, Texas The SMU Teacher Preparation Program is hiring experienced teachers and administrators to mentor the next generation of teachers in San Antonio. The Supervisor role largely requires one to: Complete online new supervisor training. (Expect information via email in early August/December.) Conduct an orientation for the cooperating teacher before the clinical teaching semester begins. This session should provide information concerning the program and required responsibilities. Conduct a clinical teacher orientation by the first day of the clinical teaching semester. Primarily, this session should review the Handbook. Monitor clinical teacher's notebook during the clinical teaching semester. Observe, assess, and evaluate the clinical teacher regarding teaching, planning, management, and professionalism (3 formal 45-minute observations per semester). Conduct mid-point and summative 3-way conference with the clinical teacher and cooperating teacher. Write recommendation letters. Act as a liaison between the school district and Teacher Preparation Program. A qualified candidate for this position meets the following criteria: 3 years teaching experience Master's degree Current Texas teaching certification and/or principal (mid-management) or superintendent certification Accomplished educator (as shown by student learning) Experience mentoring teachers Commitment for one academic year It is not required that the supervisor is a current district employee. We are reviewing applicants on an as needed basis. Please submit the following documents: Resume Cover letter that addresses all qualification criteria. Valid and active Educator Certificate."
    },
