    "https://apply.interfolio.com/150925": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Adjunct Professor of Teacher Preparation (Dallas)",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Dallas, Texas The Adjunct Professor of Teacher Preparation plays a crucial role in shaping the future of education by instructing and guiding aspiring teachers in their journey towards become effective educators. This position combines academic expertise with practical teaching experience to equip future educators with the necessary skills, knowledge, and strategies to excel in their position. Responsibilities: Course Instruction: Deliver engaging courses in teacher preparation, covering topics such as curriculum design, assessment techniques, and teaching methodologies. Grading and Feedback: Evaluate student assignments, projects, and assessments in a timely manner, providing constructive feedback to help students understand their strengths and areas for improvement. Maintain accurate records of student progress and performance. Communication witih Teacher Preparation Program Director: Check in several times during the semester about student progress and course progression. A qualified candidate for this position meets the following criteria: Terminal degree in Education or a closely related discipline. Availability to teach face-to-face courses in the evening. Availability to teach in Dallas. Experiece teaching in K-12 public schools. Minimum experience of one year teaching courses in a higher education institute. Demonstrated ability to collaborate with faculty to provide essential instruction in courses for certification preparation. Demonstrated experience teaching general education graduate courses. A commitment to fostering an inclusive and equitable learning environment that values diversity and promotes cultural competency among teacher candidates. For priority consideration, interested candidates are asked to upload the following documents to Interfolio by June 8th: Resume Cover letter that addresses all qualification criteria."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/170019": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Teacher Preparation Program Supervisor (Austin)",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Austin, Texas The SMU Teacher Preparation Program is hiring experienced teachers and administrators to mentor the next generation of teachers in Austin. The Supervisor role largely requires one to: Complete online new supervisor training. (Expect information via email in early August/December.) Conduct an orientation for the cooperating teacher before the clinical teaching semester begins. This session should provide information concerning the program and required responsibilities. Conduct a clinical teacher orientation by the first day of the clinical teaching semester. Primarily, this session should review the Handbook. Monitor clinical teacher's notebook during the clinical teaching semester. Observe, assess, and evaluate the clinical teacher regarding teaching, planning, management, and professionalism (3 formal 45-minute observations per semester). Conduct mid-point and summative 3-way conference with the clinical teacher and cooperating teacher. Write recommendation letters. Act as a liaison between the school district and Teacher Preparation Program. A qualified candidate for this position meets the following criteria: 3 years teaching experience Master's degree Current Texas teaching certification and/or principal (mid-management) or superintendent certification Accomplished educator (as shown by student learning) Experience mentoring teachers Commitment for one academic year It is not required that the supervisor is a current district employee. Thank you for considering this role. We are reviewing applications on an as need basis. Thus, this is an applicant pool. Please submit the following documents: Resume Cover letter that addresses all qualification criteria. Valid and active copy of Educator Certificate."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190905": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #52938 Clinical/Technical Assistant/Associate Professor of Counseling",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Dallas The Annette Caldwell Simmons School of Education and Human Development at Southern Methodist University seeks one full-time Clinical Assistant/Associate Professor in the Department of Counseling. Re sponsibilities include: Teach counseling courses and utiliz e generalist teaching skills for core counseling curriculum , which includes culturally sustaining content and strategies across the foundational curriculum, in keeping with CACREP standards. A cademic (in div idual and group) advising . Curriculum development and/or oversight. Provide c linical s upervision of students as needed related to clinical courses. Support departmental program projects and events . Provid e s ervice to the School and University . This is a renewable, non-tenured position with an initial three-year appointment beginning August 2027. Required qualifications include: A doctoral degree in counseling or counselor education (Ph.D. or Ed.D.) from a CACREP-accredited program . A doctoral degree in a related field and previous employment as a full-time faculty member in a counselor education program for a minimum of 1 full academic year before July 1, 2013. Teaching experience in counseling - related courses , including clinical courses and counseling core curriculum. Demonstrated identity as a counselor educator and clinician as evidenced by sustained participation in professional activities, scholarly activities, and service to the profession. Clinical experience in the field and clinical supervisory experience. Preferred qualifications: LPC licensure LMFT licensure Marriage, couple, and family counseling experience One to three years teaching experience To ensure full consideration for the position, application s must be received by October 31 , 202 6 ; the position s will remain open until filled. The following documents are required to be considered for the positions : Cover letter outlining teaching, scholarship, professional service, and clinical experiences as well as research experience, if applicable. Curriculum Vitae Three references, including their names, affiliations, and contact information. References will only be contacted if the candidate advances to the on-campus interview stage."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190912": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 #53617 Open Rank Clinical Professor of Applied Physiology & Health Management",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Dallas The Department of Applied Physiology & Sport Management (APSM) within the Simmons School of Education & Human Development at Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites applications for a non-tenure-track position as an Open Rank Clinical Professor in Applied Physiology & Health Management (#53617). This is a unique opportunity to join an innovative, interdisciplinary academic major. Creative energy, intellectual strength, a broad-ranging repertoire, and teaching interests are essential. The Role The successful candidate will be responsible for a faculty teaching load of 4 courses or approximately 12 credit hours per semester, including but not limited to existing courses: Applied Physiology Research and Ethics, Anatomy and Physiology, Exercise Physiology, and Biomechanics. The successful candidate will participate in the development, growth, and expansion of a new departmental Health Sciences Honors program as well as development of advanced coursework, including but not limited to: Advanced Cardiorespiratory Physiology, Environmental Physiology, and Pathophysiology. Service duties may include committee involvement (departmental/school/university), recruitment and student success, assessment/accreditation, and/or community/professional engagement. Why This Role Stands Out - A department and major with a rigorous, interdisciplinary curriculum that connects classroom instruction with practical and professional applications. - A position focused on teaching and mentoring highly engaged undergraduate students with opportunities to shape curriculum and student experiences. - An R1 research university with strong momentum, enabling collaboration across disciplines within SMU and the Dallas–Fort Worth health and wellness community. - Dallas is one of the fastest-growing large metropolitan areas in the country with a relatively affordable cost of living, no state income tax, two major airports, a lively restaurant scene, professional sports, and a vibrant cultural life. Minimum Qualifications - Doctoral or terminal degree in Exercise Physiology, Human Physiology, Public Health, Health Promotion/Management, or a related field by May 2027. - Ability to teach undergraduate physiology, biomechanics, and exercise science courses - Demonstrated teacher-scholar potential - Evidence of academic or community service Prefered Qualifications - Earned doctorate in a related field - Relevant undergraduate or graduate teaching experience - Postdoctoral training and evidence of scholarly research - Service or collaboration in health or exercise science. About the Department, School, and SMU The Department of Applied Physiology & Sport Management (APSM) prepares students for careers and advanced study in health, human performance, sport management, and related fields through interdisciplinary academic programs that connect science, health, and professional practice. Faculty emphasize high-quality teaching, student mentorship, research-informed practice, and experiential learning. To learn more about the department, please visit: https://www.smu.edu/simmons/academics/applied-physiology-and-sport-management. SMU’s Simmons School of Education and Human Development promotes academic excellence through preparing exemplary professionals, engaging in research, securing significant external grants and contracts, and collaborating with community partners. Founded in 2005, Simmons offers undergraduate and graduate degrees across five diverse academic departments: Applied Physiology and Sport Management, Counseling, Education Policy and Leadership, Human-Centered Interdisciplinary Studies, and Teaching and Learning. SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan areas. Faculty are part of an inclusive and intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. To learn more about the rich cultural environment of SMU, please see: www.smu.edu. All materials should be submitted electronically through Interfolio and should include: (1) A cover letter outlining teaching, service experience, and related qualifications; (2) A curriculum vitae; (3) Names, affiliations, and contact information for three references. References will only be contacted if the candidate advances to the on-campus interview stage. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 15, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. The start date for this position is August 2027. Salary is competitive and commensurate with qualifications and experience. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to interview and give a teaching demonstration. For questions about the department or position, please contact Dr. Scott Davis, Search Committee Chair, at sldavis@smu.edu. For questions about the search submission process, please contact Susan Sifford at ssifford@smu.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193787": {
      "title": "SMU Simmons Postdoctoral Fellow in Education Research – Quantitative Analyses #00054896",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Location: Simmons School of Education and Human Development Priority Consideration Date : Review of applications will begin October 5, 2026. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 5, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. The Research in Mathematics Education (RME) unit at Southern Methodist University seeks a full-time postdoctoral research associate to conduct research related to Tier 2 mathematics interventions implemented with students experiencing mathematics difficulty (MD). The successful candidate will join an interdisciplinary team of education researchers and practitioners. They will play a key role in studying student and interventionist interactions with a Tier 2 fraction intervention and the outcomes of participating in the intervention program. The available data covers multiple clustered randomized controlled trials with elementary and middle school students and multiple outcome measures related to mathematics and motivation. Examples of targeted areas for scholarship include mathematics education, Multi-Tiered Systems of Support (MTSS), development of intervention programs, and measurement and assessment. The postdoctoral researcher will have opportunities to collaborate with faculty, graduate students, and project staff from all project sites (Southern Methodist University, University of Texas at Austin, University of Missouri, and American Institutes for Research). The postdoctoral researcher will be expected to develop and lead their own complementary research activities aligned with the scope of the research unit. The postdoctoral research associate will have expertise in education, mathematics, statistics and/or psychometrics. Applicants must demonstrate promise for a tenure-track appointment at a research college or university. Statement of Duties and Responsibilities Support implementation of research activities for the U.S. Department of Education Office of Education Innovation and Research-funded project: S411B210032. Mentor graduate and undergraduate students, oversee data organization and processing, participate in communication with the external evaluation team. Participate in the analysis of mainly quantitative student and interventionist assessment, interventionist training, and implementation data. Contribute to manuscript preparation, grant-related reporting, and other dissemination efforts. Conduct research complementary to the existing research activities to establish one's own research agenda. This is an in-person position at the Southern Methodist Campus in Dallas, TX Salary and Appointment The initial appointment is for one year with the possibility of renewal for an additional year, based on satisfactory performance. The preferred start date is November 1, 2026. The salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and relevant research. Minimum Qualifications The initial appointment is for one year with the possibility of renewal for an additional year, based on satisfactory performance. The preferred start date is November 1, 2026. The salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and relevant research. Applicants must have completed requirements for a doctoral or other terminal degree in education, learning sciences, mathematics education, statistics, or related fields within the past five years or have evidence of completion of all requirements for the doctoral degree by the start date, including successfully defended dissertation. Evidence of scholarly activity/potential is required. Preferred Qualifications Content expertise in mathematics, education, and/or statistics. Experience in MTSS and assessment. Interest and knowledge in quantitative methods, including multivariate statistics, multi-level modeling, psychometrics, and/or meta-analysis. Teaching experience in primary or middle school grades. Research and publications related to mathematics, MTSS, educational psychology, or mathematics education. Demonstrated commitment to mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. Submission Materials · Application · CV · Cover letter should include detailed information about qualifications for this position, including a clear statement of research interests, relevant content/subject area, and/or data analysis/management experiences. · Provide a statement that confirms (a) that requirements for a doctoral or terminal degree will have been successfully completed by the start date (b) confirms your understanding that this position is in-person in Dallas, TX and (c) describes your experience in mathematics education · 1-3 relevant publications with a description of applicant’s contribution · 3-5 professional references, including doctoral dissertation advisor (Letters may be requested.) Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. SMU Nondiscrimination Statement: SMU is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187165": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 #6050 - O'Donnell Eminent Endowed Chair in Energy (Position 00006050)",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Roy M. Huffington Department in Earth Sciences O'Donnell Eminent Endowed Chair in Energy - Position 00006050 Southern Methodist University invites applications to the inaugural O'Donnell Eminent Endowed Chair and Professorship in Energy. The position is a key component of a new $50M O'Donnell Energy Initiative at SMU. The candidate would hold a leadership position across programs spanning Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, the Lyle School of Engineering, and the Cox School of Business at the rank of tenured, full professor. This appointment is focused on establishing SMU as a global innovator in energy solutions. The Chair will work to facilitate world-class research addressing energy challenges with the goal of forging new and innovative avenues of investigation across SMU. The Chair will also provide direction on undergraduate and graduate degree programs, with initial focus on an interdisciplinary and cross-school BS honors program on Energy Science, Innovation and Enterprise. The candidate is expected to develop a research and teaching portfolio focused on energy with emphasis on petroleum systems. The ideal candidate will be able to build partnerships with industry, government, and national laboratories. The successful candidate is expected to join the dynamic Roy M. Huffington Department of Earth Sciences, whose internationally recognized faculty conducts collaborative, societally relevant research in energy, environmental systems, and earth hazards that links to complementary strengths within the university. The Department core facilities include Stable Isotope and X-ray Analytical laboratories, Geophysical and Remote Sensing facilities and instrumentation, and the Shuler Museum of Paleontology. SMU interdisciplinary expertise in energy systems is further enabled through state-of-the-art High Performance Computing facilities; the O'Donnell Data Science Institute; the Maguire Energy Institute; cross-school research excellence in earth hazards and national security, subsurface enregy and law, the mathematics of inversion, imaging and machine learning, advanced energy storage, decarbonization and monitoring, and subsurface fluid and heat transport systems. Joint appointments across other departments or schools would be welcomed. SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation's fastest growing metropolitan areas. Teachers and researchers are part of an inclusive and intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, engineering, and business. SMU seeks colleagues who eagerly embrace SMU's central values of intellectual diversity, cultural intelligence and academic freedom. SMU offers excellent benefits including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation's most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers, and the Dallas-Fort Worth metroplex is the fourth largest in the US. Dallas' quality of life is exceptional, with a relatively low cost of living and a variety of housing options. Neighborhoods within easy commute of campus offer tree-lined streets and walkability with easy access to the city’s dynamic arts, music, sports and dining scenes that combine to make Dallas an extremely livable city. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. The successful applicant will have a demonstrated ability to maintain an outstanding, internationally recognized, externally funded research program; to teach, lead, and collaborate effectively with a diverse student body of graduates and undergraduates; and to supervise professional staff and faculty. All faculty are expected to fully participate in the educational mission of the university. The position will start January 2, 2027. Candidates must have the following qualifications: A PhD in the geosciences or closely related field A research record commensurate with an appoinment as a tenured full professor, with more than six years post PhD experience. Demonstrated ability to establish an internationally recognized, externally funded research program A commitment to excellence in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Applications recieved by September 15, 2026, will receive full consideration, but applications will be reviewed as received and continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Applications should include a cover letter that discusses leadership experience in energy systems with an emphasis on petroleum, CV, statements on research and teaching, and contact information for three letters of recommendation. Please submit application materials via interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/187165. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. For more information, please visit the Roy M. Huffington Department of Earth Sciences, or contact Heather DeShon, Professor and Department Chair in Earth Sciences at geology@smu.edu. SMU is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193114": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 Assistant Teaching Professor / Program Director in Jewish Studies",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Religious Studies in Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a non-tenure-track position in Jewish Studies at the rank of Assistant Teaching Professor. The position requires residency in Dallas beginning August 1, 2027. The position is renewable after three and/or five years with the possibility of promotion. The teaching load is three classes in each of the fall and the spring semesters. Department of Religious Studies: The Department of Religious Studies at SMU is a dynamic, exceptionally collegial group of researchers and teachers with intellectual breadth that spans the globe, multiple academic disciplines, and several thousand years of history. Our research has been recognized with numerous fellowships, grants, and awards, including a Guggenheim Fellowship, a Templeton Foundation grant, and several national and international book awards. Faculty in the department are also dedicated to undergraduate and graduate teaching and have been recognized multiple times at the university, national, and international levels. Visit us at http://www.smu.edu/Academics/Departments/Religious-Studies About SMU: Located near the center of Dallas, SMU is a private, non-sectarian university of about 12,000 students offering strong undergraduate, graduate, and professional programs through seven schools – Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, Cox School of Business, Meadows School of the Arts, Lyle School of Engineering, Perkins School of Theology, Dedman School of Law, and Simmons School of Education and Human Development. To learn more about the rich and vibrant culture of SMU, please visit www.smu.edu. Minimum Qualifications : Ph.D. or ABD in Jewish Studies or related field. Ability to teach undergraduate courses, both introductory and advanced, on a wide range of topics in Jewish Studies and direct the Jewish Studies program (currently a minor). Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. in hand by the time of appointment. Expertise in some facet of Judaism/Jewish life. Competence to teach an introductory course in Jewish Studies as well as upper-level classes in Jewish history, culture, experience, religion, etc. Ability to direct a Jewish Studies minor in the department and perform relevant administrative duties including course scheduling, recruiting, advising minors, and event programming. Capacity to contribute to the Graduate Program in Religious Studies. Application : Submit letter of application highlighting the candidate’s qualifications, complete curriculum vitae, names of three recommenders, and evidence of teaching excellence (teaching philosophy statement, sample syllabi, etc.) to https://apply.interfolio.com/193114 . Review of applications will begin November 1st. To ensure full consideration for the position, the applications must be received by November 7th, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193118": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 Assistant Professor of Economics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Economics at Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the Assistant Professor level. Candidates should demonstrate a record of, or potential for, excellence in research, teaching, and Ph.D. student supervision. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds. Department . The SMU Department of Economics is home to a passionate and dedicated group of research-active scholars. Student demand for Economics at SMU is incredible. In addition to multiple undergraduate degrees, we also maintain successful master’s and Ph.D. programs. We seek individuals who will continue and accelerate the upward trajectory of the department and SMU, both in research and in the education and mentorship of undergraduates and graduate students. For more information see http://smu.edu/economics/. University . SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation’s fastest growing metropolitan areas. Researchers are part of an inclusive and intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits, including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation’s most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers, and the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex is the fourth largest in the US. Dallas’ quality of life is exceptional, with a relatively low cost of living and a variety of housing options. A full range of professional sports teams, music venues, a lively and innovative restaurant scene, and more make Dallas an extremely livable city. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. A Ph.D. in Economics or a related field is required by the August 1, 2027 start date. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio ( https://apply.interfolio.com/193118 ). Candidates should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a sample of recent research, a research statement, and three letters of recommendation. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 15, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. For additional information on this position, send inquiries to eco@smu.edu. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193122": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 Assistant Teaching Professor of Economics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Economics at Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites applications for a non-tenure track Assistant Teaching Professor. We are interested in candidates with teaching expertise and interest in all areas of economics. The position entails a a three-year contract with the possibility of renewal and a typical 4-4 teaching load (that includes teaching-related departmental activities), and no research requirements. Teaching responsibilities will primarily be undergraduate courses with the possibility of a limited number of masters’ courses. The successful candidate should be ableto teach introductory and intermediate microeconomics and macroeconomics, as well as advanced courses in their area of expertise. We seek an innovative and passionate educator and mentor for undergraduates and graduate students. There is also the possibility of a second position for a candidate with a particular interest in teaching sports economics. Department . The SMU Department of Economics is home to an exciting group of researchactive scholars, lecturers, and staff. All faculty are dedicated instructors. Economics is the most popular major in Dedman College among undergraduates. We also maintain successful master’s and Ph.D. programs. For more information, see http://smu.edu/economics/. University. SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation’s fastest growing metropolitan areas. Researchers are part of an inclusive and intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits, including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation’s most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers, and the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex is the fourth largest in the US. Dallas’ quality of life is exceptional, with a relatively low cost of living and a variety of housing options. A full range of professional sports teams, music venues, a lively and innovative restaurant scene, and more make Dallas an extremely livable city. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. Minimum Qualifications. A Ph.D. in Economics or a related field is required by the starting date of August 1, 2027. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193122 . Candidates should upload a cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, and three letters of recommendation. Please state in your cover letter when you expect to complete your Ph.D. if you have yet to do so. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 1, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. For additional information on this position, send inquiries to eco@smu.edu. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193136": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 Open Rank Teaching Professor of Statistics and Data Science",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 10, 2026 Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Statistics and Data Science at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track, open-rank teaching professor position beginning August 1, 2027. The appointment may be made at the rank of Teaching Assistant Professor, Teaching Associate Professor, or Teaching Professor, commensurate with the candidate's qualifications and experience. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in statistics and data science; contribute to curriculum development, course coordination, and continuous improvement of instructional programs; mentor students; and participate in departmental, college, and university service. The department values excellent instruction, thoughtful use of modern computational tools, and the ability to connect statistical foundations with contemporary data science practice. An important component of the position will be developing and teaching courses in artificial intelligence and its applications to statistics and data science, including machine learning and generative AI. The successful candidate will also help integrate the responsible, rigorous, and reproducible use of AI methods and tools across the statistics and data science curriculum. Department and University The Department of Statistics and Data Science is the academic home for research and teaching in statistics and data science at SMU. The department offers bachelor's degrees in Statistical Science and Data Science, the MS in Data Science and Applied Statistics, and the online Master of Science in Data Science. Its doctoral offerings comprise the PhD in Statistics and Data Science and the PhD in Biostatistics. Faculty teach and mentor students across these programs and collaborate throughout the University and the Dallas-Fort Worth region. Additional information is available at smu.edu/statistics. Southern Methodist University is a nationally ranked private research university in Dallas, Texas. SMU offers a collegial academic environment, a broad interdisciplinary community, and access to the professional, cultural, and technological resources of the Dallas-Fort Worth metropolitan area. Minimum Qualifications A PhD or equivalent terminal degree in statistics, data science, biostatistics, or a closely related quantitative field, completed by August 1, 2027. Demonstrated experience teaching statistics, data science, or closely related quantitative courses at the college level. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and the ability to teach a broad range of courses using modern statistical computing tools. Effective written and oral communication skills and the ability to mentor students from varied academic backgrounds. For appointment above the assistant rank, a sustained record of teaching excellence, curricular contribution, leadership, and service commensurate with the proposed rank. Preferred Qualifications Experience developing or coordinating statistics or data science curricula. Experience with applied, collaborative, consulting, or industry-facing data science work. Experience teaching with reproducible computing workflows and current software environments. Experience developing or teaching courses in artificial intelligence, machine learning, or generative AI, particularly with applications to statistics and data science. Evidence of effective student mentoring, program leadership, or innovative pedagogy. Applicants should submit the following materials: A letter of application describing qualifications and interest in the position. A curriculum vitae. A teaching statement describing teaching philosophy and experience. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as teaching evaluations, sample syllabi, or representative instructional materials. Three confidential letters of reference. Applications must be submitted through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193136 . The priority deadline is October 16, 2026, and the position will remain open to applications until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to Raanju Sundararajan at rsundararajan@smu.edu. Employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193610": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 #00005738 - Professor of History and Department Chair",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites applications and nominations for an accomplished scholar and academic leader to serve as Chair of the Department of History during a period of significant growth and opportunity. The successful candidate will hold a tenured appointment at the rank of Professor and will assume the chair position beginning August 1, 2027 . The field of academic specialization is open. The Department of History is comprised of a vibrant community of scholars committed to excellence in research, teaching, and service. It supports robust programs for majors and minors, in addition to meeting vital curricular needs across the campus. The department has a strong PhD program in United States history, to which all members of the department in and out of that field contribute. It also offers MAs in both US and Global history. It collaborates closely with the Clements Center for Southwest Studies and the Center for Presidential History.  The University provides access to first-rate archival collections, including the new Rees-Jones Library for the American West, DeGolyer Library, Bridwell Library, and the George W. Bush Presidential Library. The next chair will have an exceptional opportunity to work with faculty, students, college leadership, and university partners to advance the department's academic mission and long-term vision. About SMU and Dedman College SMU is a nationally recognized, Carnegie R1, private research university located in Dallas, Texas. With approximately 12,000 students drawn from across the United States and around the world, SMU provides students with small classes, as well as meaningful opportunities for research and leadership and an intellectually engaging education. The University is home to eight degree-granting schools and colleges, creating substantial opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and innovation. Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences is the academic heart of SMU and encompasses a broad range of disciplines in the humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, and mathematics. The College is committed to advancing knowledge through outstanding scholarship while providing students with a rigorous and transformative liberal arts and sciences education. Located in the dynamic Dallas–Fort Worth region, SMU offers faculty an exceptional environment for research, teaching, collaboration, and engagement with one of the nation's most rapidly growing metropolitan areas. The Opportunity The Department Chair will provide strategic, academic, and administrative leadership for the department while working collaboratively with faculty, staff, students, the Dean, and university leadership. The successful candidate will be expected to: Collaboratively build and implement an ambitious vision for the department's academic and scholarly future; Support excellence in research, graduate education, and undergraduate education. Work with faculty and the Dean to identify strategic priorities for faculty hiring and program development; Advocate effectively for the department and represent it within the College and University; Recruit, mentor, and retain outstanding faculty; support the professional development of junior scholars; and support external funding opportunities for faculty at all levels; Oversee departmental budgets, resources, personnel, and academic programs in collaboration with college leadership; Foster a collegial, inclusive, and intellectually vibrant departmental culture. Encourage interdisciplinary research and collaboration within Dedman College and across SMU; and Engage constructively with students and contribute to the broader educational mission of SMU. The chair will join a university committed to intellectual diversity, academic freedom, rigorous inquiry, and the development of students as ethical and engaged leaders. Candidates must hold an earned Ph.D. and have a distinguished record of scholarly achievement commensurate with appointment at the rank of tenured Professor. Successful candidates will demonstrate: A distinguished record of research, scholarship, or creative achievement in the field of history; Demonstrated academic leadership and administrative experience; Demonstrated excellence in advising and mentoring graduate students, including doctoral students, and interest in making significant contributions to the department’s graduate program; Demonstrated excellence in teaching at the undergraduate level; A record of effective faculty collaboration and shared governance; The ability to articulate a compelling vision for the future of the department; and The interpersonal and organizational skills necessary to lead an academic department effectively. Candidates with experience in interdisciplinary research, program development, external funding, faculty recruitment, or strategic academic planning are especially encouraged to apply. Applicants should submit the following materials through https://apply.interfolio.com/193610. A cover letter describing the candidate's qualifications for the position, research and scholarly accomplishments, teaching experience, and vision for leadership of the Department of History; Curriculum vitae; A statement of leadership philosophy and administrative experience; and Contact information for three professional references. Applications received by October 30, 2026 will receive full consideration. The search will remain open until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is August 1, 2027. Questions regarding the position may be directed to Professor Katherine Carté, kecarte@smu.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193706": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Associate or Full Professor of Economics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Economics at Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites applications for a tenured faculty position at the Associate or Full Professor level. The successful candidate is expected to be a preeminent expert in their field, with a strong record of Ph.D. student supervision and an interest in helping to further grow and expand the Department in their area of expertise. We welcome candidates from all backgrounds. Department. The SMU Department of Economics is home to a passionate and dedicated group of research-active scholars. Student demand for Economics at SMU is booming. In addition to multiple undergraduate degrees, we also maintain successful master’s and Ph.D. programs. We seek individuals who will continue and accelerate the upward trajectory of the department and SMU, both in research and in the education and mentorship of undergraduates and graduate students. For more information, see http://smu.edu/economics/. University . SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation’s fastest growing metropolitan areas. Researchers are part of an inclusive and intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits, including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation’s most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers, and the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex is the fourth largest in the US. Dallas’ quality of life is exceptional, with a relatively low cost of living and a variety of housing options. A full range of professional sports teams, music venues, a lively and innovative restaurant scene, and more make Dallas an extremely livable city. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. A Ph.D. in Economics or a related field is required prior to the start of employment. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio (https://apply.interfolio.com/193706). Candidates should upload a cover letter, a curriculum vitae, a sample of recent research, a research statement, and provide the names of at least three references (letters not required for the initial application). Applications will be evaluated on a rolling basis, starting on October 16, 2026, and continuing until the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Candidates who want to express an interest or have further questions should contact Klaus Desmet at kdesmet@smu.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193708": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Department Chair and Professor of Physics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Physics at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a tenured senior faculty member to serve as department chair beginning August 1, 2027. We seek an accomplished experimental physicist with an internationally recognized research program and a record of scientific leadership who can help shape the Department’s next phase of research growth. Candidates from experimental physics and closely aligned areas that complement or extend our existing research strengths are encouraged to apply. We are particularly interested in scholars whose work involves advanced instrumentation, detector or sensor development, electronics, photonics, or related technologies and who see opportunities to build upon SMU’s established experimental infrastructure and multidisciplinary research capabilities. Research : Southern Methodist University is an R1 research-intensive, private liberal arts university centrally located in the vibrant Dallas-Fort Worth metropolitan area. The Department pursues an ambitious research agenda focused on the most pressing and transformative questions in physics. Efforts span high-energy physics, nuclear physics, astrophysics, and cosmology. We participate in a diverse range of large, international collaborations, including ATLAS and CMS, the Electron-Ion Collider, NOvA, ICARUS, DESI, the Simons Observatory, the Vera C. Rubin Observatory, and COMAP, among others. Within ATLAS, our research focuses primarily on Higgs boson and multi-boson physics, spanning precision studies of Standard Model processes and searches for new phenomena. The team also plays a major role in the development, upgrade, and maintenance of the ATLAS liquid argon calorimeter, the trigger, and the pixel detector. The work at SMU spans R&D in high-speed, radiation-hard optical links, chip design, and firmware development for off-detector readout electronics. The astrophysics and cosmology program explores dark energy, dark matter, and gravity through galaxy clustering, gravitational lensing, the cosmic microwave background, line-intensity mapping, and supernovae, and includes a detector R&D program in photonics. Our department’s theory and high-energy/nuclear groups pursue collider phenomenology, higher-order QCD calculations, and precision determinations of proton and nuclear structure. Resources : SMU supports the Department’s research with substantial resources, including extensive computational capabilities provided by the O’Donnell Data Science and Research Computing Institute. A multifaceted high-performance cluster supports large-scale numerical and computer-algebra methods; an NVIDIA SuperPOD enables advanced HPC and AI/ML research; and high-memory nodes support massive, high-resolution N-body cosmological simulations. The Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies supports our Ph.D. program and provides pathways for specialization across our research areas. The Dedman College Interdisciplinary Institute supports undergraduate study in our degree programs, which include the BS and BA in physics with an optional astrophysics specialization, and the BS in Biophysical Sciences. The successful candidate for Chair will bring a record of scientific and strategic leadership, along with demonstrated experience leading a large, diverse, and collaborative organization of multidisciplinary scientists and engineers. They will provide visionary leadership to define and advance the Department’s teaching and research priorities, strengthen undergraduate and graduate education, expand externally funded research programs, and foster ambitious, interdisciplinary collaborations. The position calls for substantial experience with large-scale budget management, personnel oversight, and the evaluation of tenure-line faculty. The Chair will foster collaborative research agendas that strategically leverage SMU’s resources and core strengths, advancing innovative scholarship while furthering the University’s mission as a premier private urban research institution. The Chair will also maintain a vigorous, impactful, externally funded research program that builds upon the Department’s strong culture of scientific excellence. This search seeks applicants broadly in physics, and we welcome candidates with connections to existing experimental and observational programs. We particularly encourage applicants who can leverage SMU’s resources, including the Department’s established optoelectronics infrastructure, to develop new research directions and expand existing strengths. Interested candidates are invited to submit a curriculum vitae and a statement describing their research accomplishments, scientific leadership, and executive or administrative experience. Please submit applications through https://apply.interfolio.com/193708. Review of applications will begin November 10, 2026, but applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Applicants will be notified of the committee’s decision following the completion of the search. Employment is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Questions or inquiries may be directed to the search chair, Prof. Fredrick Olness, at olness@smu.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193843": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Associate Professor of Archaeology",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Anthropology at Southern Methodist University invites applications for the position of an Associate Professor (with tenure) with a focus on environmental archaeology. We seek specialists with an outstanding record of community-based and locally engaged research and scholarship in archaeological sciences. Preference will be given to those with methodological expertise in geochemical or molecular methods that complement existing expertise in the department (for example biomarkers, proteomics, stable isotopes, and/or ZooMS). Topical expertise is open, but we are particularly interested in scholars with established strengths in the climatic and environmental contexts of human and social evolution, conceived broadly. Strong consideration will be given to anthropologists whose scholarship and teaching complement the regional and topical expertise of current faculty in environmental archaeology and environmental anthropology and bridge cognate campus programs. Successful applicants will have evidence of an ongoing research program, evidence of teaching effectiveness. Regional focus is open. Applicants at the rank of Associate Professor are preferred but Assistant Professors nearing promotion and tenure will be considered. The position begins August 1, 2027. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to be a significant partner in building ties between the department and a globally connected community at the university and in the Dallas-Fort Worth Metroplex. They will also join a fast-growing, PhD-granting department that participates actively in interdisciplinary initiatives on campus through the Dedman College Interdisciplinary Institute, which features ongoing scholarly clusters in Earth Hazards and National Security, Energy and Environmental Systems, Tribal Sovereignty and Natural Resources, Asian Studies and Asian American Experiences, and Urban Research. More information on the Dedman College Interdisciplinary Institute research clusters can be found at https://www.smu.edu/dedman/research/institutes-and-centers/dcii/research/research-cluster. Department/Division: The Department of Anthropology at SMU seeks to recruit, develop, and retain the most talented people from a wide candidate pool. Accordingly, we prefer candidates who work productively with colleagues, within and across disciplines, and are skilled at communicating with multiple audiences using different forms of media. Beyond research, the successful candidate must also demonstrate a firm commitment to teaching and mentorship in a liberal arts environment. Visit us at https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/anthropology. SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in one of the nation’s fastest growing metropolitan areas. Researchers are part of an intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits including full same-sex domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation’s most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers, and the Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex is the fourth largest in the US. Dallas’ quality of life is exceptional, with a relatively low cost of living and a variety of housing options. A full range of professional sports teams, music venues, a lively and innovative restaurant scene, and more make Dallas an extremely livable city. Explore Dedman College at https://www.smu.edu/dedman, and SMU at http://www.smu.edu. Minimum qualifications: PhD in Anthropology or related field; expertise to teach courses in anthropology, specifically anthropological and archaeological science approaches to environmental issues and questions. Preferred qualifications : Current rank of Associate Professor (or equivalent); experience teaching undergraduate or graduate courses in anthropology as well as courses in any of the following areas: environmental archaeology, archaeological sciences, paleoclimatology and/or other aspects of ancient human-environment dynamics; interest in making significant contributions to graduate education; an ambitious research agenda and scholarship which has resulted in successful grant awards, publications in peer-reviewed journals, conference presentations, and invited talks; experience mentoring a diverse student population. Submit a cover letter highlighting qualifications, a complete curriculum vitae, a research statement, and the names of three references via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193843. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 1, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Questions regarding the application should be directed to Dr. Christopher Roos at croos@smu.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193856": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Assistant Professor of Psychology - Psychological Clinical Science",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas Applications are invited for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Psychological Clinical Science at Southern Methodist University to begin August 1, 2027. The successful candidate will contribute meaningfully to our doctoral program in clinical science, which is a member of the Academy of Psychological Clinical Science and accredited by the American Psychological Association. Candidates should demonstrate the potential to establish and maintain a rigorous and highly productive research program and obtain external funding. Candidates also must be committed to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the graduate and undergraduate levels. We especially welcome candidates whose research complements existing strengths within the Department of Psychology, while bringing new theoretical or methodological perspectives to our clinical science program. The candidate will have opportunities to collaborate with researchers across the University and to develop research programs that take advantage of SMU's strengths in data science, computing, and related areas, such as through the O'Donnell Data Science and Research Computing Institute. For additional information about our faculty, the doctoral program, and the department, visit https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/psychology. SMU is a nationally ranked, private, non-sectarian research institution located in University Park (https://www.uptexas.org) in the rapidly growing Dallas–Fort Worth metropolitan area. SMU brings together an intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social and life sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business, creating opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration and research across traditional disciplinary boundaries. The University is entering an important period of growth and strategic development under new leadership, with a renewed institutional agenda focused on research excellence, interdisciplinary collaboration, innovation, and academic distinction. This creates a particularly exciting environment for new faculty to contribute to and help shape the next phase of SMU’s academic and research trajectory. The Dallas–Fort Worth region offers an exceptional network of academic, medical, business, and community resources, including major academic medical centers such as UT Southwestern Medical Center and Baylor Scott & White Health, and a wide range of universities, healthcare organizations, businesses, and community partners. These resources provide opportunities for research and collaboration spanning psychological science, medicine, health, technology, business, and community-based research. SMU offers excellent benefits, including benefits for eligible spouses and domestic partners. Explore Dedman College at https://www.smu.edu/dedman , and SMU at http://www.smu.edu. The Department of Psychology is seeking candidates with a strong and innovative program of research addressing important questions in Psychological Clinical Science. We welcome applicants working across the full range of clinical science, including, but not limited to, etiology, mechanisms, prevention, assessment, and treatment of psychopathology (broadly defined). Candidates with doctoral training in closely related areas of psychology whose research programs are strongly aligned with Psychological Clinical Science are also encouraged to apply; however, preference will be given to applicants with a Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio to https://apply.interfolio.com/193856. Interested applicants should submit: Cover letter describing the candidate's research program, fit with the Department of Psychology and the Psychological Clinical Science doctoral program, and contributions to teaching and mentoring; Curriculum vitae; Statement of research; Statement of teaching philosophy and evidence of teaching effectiveness; Three letters of reference; and Up to three representative publications or preprints. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by October 18, 2026; however, the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. For questions about the search submission process or position, contact Dr. Alicia E. Meuret (ameuret@smu.edu), Psychology Search Committee Chair. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193858": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Associate or Full Professor in Psychological Science",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas, TX The Department of Psychology at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a tenure-track Associate or Full Professor position in Psychological Clinical Science to begin on August 1, 2027. We are searching for a candidate with an outstanding research record to serve as the Director of Clinical Training in our Psychological Clinical Science Ph.D. program. Our APA accredited program is a member of the Academy of Psychological Clinical Science. Accreditation by the Psychological Clinical Science Accreditation System (PCSAS) is a goal of our program. Therefore, the ideal candidate will have previous academic leadership experience and knowledge regarding PCSAS and the Clinical Science training model. For additional information about our doctoral program and the department, please visit our website: https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/psychology The specific area of research is open, as long as it is within psychological clinical science. The ideal candidate will have an active program of research that can both complement and extend existing faculty strengths, and a track record of successfully generating external funding for research. Candidates also must be committed to excellence in teaching and mentoring at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and a track record of having mentored psychological clinical scientists. SMU is an intellectually vibrant community of teachers and scholars who value robust research and creative agendas. Explore SMU at www.smu.edu. Our beautifully shaded campus of Georgian-Revival-inspired architecture is situated in the heart of Dallas. The Dallas/Fort Worth Metroplex, a culturally rich arts and global business center, is home to many universities, arts organizations and Fortune 500 and over 100 corporations. Visit www.dallaschamber.org. Explore Dedman College at https://www.smu.edu/dedman and SMU at http://www.smu.edu. The Department of Psychology runs a training clinic, which can be used for research in assessment and treatment. The University is entering an important period of growth and strategic development under new leadership, with a renewed institutional agenda focused on research excellence, interdisciplinary collaboration, innovation, and academic distinction. This creates a particularly exciting environment for new faculty to contribute to and help shape the next phase of SMU’s academic and research trajectory. The Moody School of Graduate and Advanced Studies offers many forms of support for doctoral students, including a range of research fellowships. SMU is also home to one of the fastest and most powerful academic supercomputers in the nation (ManeFrame III), which can handle large volumes of complex data as well as the ability to implement advanced algorithms for time-series analysis, data mining, computational modeling, AI, and machine learning. More information is available here: https://www.smu.edu/provost/odonnell-institute A Ph.D. is required for the position. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193858. Interested applicants should submit a cover letter highlighting the candidate’s qualifications; a curriculum vitae; a statement of research; a statement of teaching philosophy and interests, evidence of teaching and mentoring effectiveness, up to 5 representative publications or preprints, and contact information for three professional references who may be contacted at a later date. To ensure full consideration, applications should be received by December 1, 2026; however, the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. For questions about the search submission process or position, contact Ernie Jouriles, Ph.D., ejourile@smu.edu, Psychology Search Committee Chair. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Start date is August 1, 2027."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193859": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Department Chair and Professor of Political Science",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Political Science Department at Southern Methodist University invites applications for the Endowed Geurin-Pettus Distinguished Chair in American Politics and Political Economy. The successful candidate will also serve as Departmental Chair. Preference will be given to senior scholars with outstanding records of research and teaching in American National Politics and public policy with emphasis on regulatory, fiscal, and social welfare issues. They will be expected to participate in an interdisciplinary program in public policy, which is administered by the Political Science Department, and will be a member of the John G. Tower Center for Public Policy and International Affairs. The Political Science Department at SMU is currently a faculty of sixteen administering an undergraduate program with 225 majors and 150 minors. Classes are small and expectations are high in both teaching and research. The Tower Center, housed with the Political Science Department, is an interdisciplinary center sponsoring and administering teaching, research, professional presentations, and conferences on national and international affairs. The successful candidate will be expected to take a leading role in developing the department, contributing to the Tower Center, and coordinating their related programs. SMU seeks bold, curious and creative faculty to position our students for success. We offer the rare confluence of a comprehensive global research institution and a close-knit intellectual enterprise in the liberal arts tradition. Like our hometown of Dallas, our campus is vibrant. Here, innovative thinkers conduct research that has lasting impact and transform big ideas into new products, inventive technology and world-changing solutions. Our welcoming learning community of faculty and over 12,000 students in eight schools embraces a world of people and ideas. Come to SMU to think big and do good. A Ph.D. is required at the time of application. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio (https://apply.interfolio.com/193859) and should include a letter highlighting the candidate’s qualifications, complete curriculum vitae, and three letters of recommendation. The position begins on August 1, 2027. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by January 15, 2027, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193862": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Associate or Full Professor of History of the American West and Director of the Clements Center for Southwest Studies",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Clements Department of History at Southern Methodist University invites outstanding applicants for the directorship of the William P. Clements Center for Southwest Studies and a tenured appointment in the History Department at the rank of Full or Associate Professor positioned for promotion in the near term. Fields of expertise may include the North American West, U.S. Southwest, U.S.-Mexico borderlands, and/or related fields. The position offers unparalleled opportunities for research, scholarly community, and teaching. The Clements Center for Southwest Studies, established in 1996, hosts a competitive fellowship program, nationally regarded symposia series, collaborative seminars, and public programming. It also sponsors the David J. Weber book prize with the Western Historical Association. The director will support fellows and emerging scholars, develop collaborative programming, cultivate community, and further enhance the Center's national and international reputation. The director will be an integral member of the Department of History. The successful candidate will foster close integration between the Center’s programs and the broader academic mission of the Department of History. Candidates should be prepared to advise and direct graduate students and to teach undergraduate and graduate classes in relevant fields. The successful candidate will benefit from engagement with the Rees-Jones Library of the American West at SMU. The new library, expected to open in Fall 2027, will be home to one of the most significant collections of Western Americana in the country. While the Rees-Jones Collection is focused primarily on the pre-1901 period, the 20th-century holdings of SMU’s DeGolyer Library include Western business enterprises and literature, political collections, and the Archives of Women of the Southwest and Mexican history. The Bridwell Library, the Bridwell Press, the Meadows Museum (a leading center in the United States for exhibition, research and education in the arts and culture of Spain), and the Archaeological Research Center offer other opportunities for exploring diverse histories of the West on SMU’s campus. About the Department: The Clements Department of History has 15 full-time faculty members—award-winning scholars and several Altshuler Distinguished Teacher professors (the highest teaching recognition on campus). Our faculty teach undergraduate courses that serve history majors and minors, as well as a number of interdisciplinary majors on campus and the university Common Curriculum. The department’s graduate program emphasizes work on the southwestern United States, the West, ethnic Mexicans, U.S. political and diplomatic history, colonial era, and religious history. The department’s centers, the Clements Center for Southwest Studies and the Center for Presidential History, enrich department life with research colloquia, public lectures, and postdoctoral fellowships. SMU and Dallas: SMU offers an exceptional setting for a scholar of the American West. SMU is a private, nonsectarian university of about 12,000 students located in University Park, a highly regarded residential community approximately 15 minutes north of downtown Dallas. The University is home to numerous academic centers with which our faculty engage, including the Texas-Mexico Center, the Dallas Institute of Humanities and Culture, the John G. Tower Center for Political Studies, and the Dedman College Interdisciplinary Institute. Our faculty take advantage of opportunities to teach at SMU’s Taos campus and its study abroad programs. Beyond the University, the Dallas–Fort Worth metropolitan area offers the cultural resources of one of the nation's largest and most diverse metropolitan regions, while providing close connections to the landscapes, archives, institutions, and communities central to the history of Texas and the American West. Opportunities abound to engage with organizations, institutions, and historical collections throughout the region, including the Dallas Historical Society, the Old Red Museum of Dallas County History & Culture, the African American Museum, the Sixth Floor Museum at Dealey Plaza, the Dallas Public Library’s Texas/Dallas History & Archives Division, and the George W. Bush Presidential Library and Museum. The broader North Texas region also offers access to the Kimbell Art Museum, the Amon Carter Museum of American Art, the Sid Richardson Museum, and the National Cowgirl Museum and Hall of Fame, among other institutions with significant collections and programming related to western history and culture. Learn more about working at SMU here and living in Dallas here . The successful applicant will demonstrate the following minimum qualifications: A distinguished and sustained record of scholarly publication and intellectual leadership in the history of the North American West, U.S. Southwest, U.S.-Mexico borderlands, or a closely related field appropriate to appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor or Associate Professor, positioned for promotion in the near term. A record of excellence in teaching. A record of professional and/or institutional service and the capacity to provide intellectual and administrative leadership appropriate to the directorship of a major research center. Preferred qualifications: Demonstrated experience in advising and mentoring graduate students, including doctoral students and interest in making significant contributions to the department’s graduate program. Applications must be submitted via Interfolio (https://apply.interfolio.com/193862) and should include a letter highlighting the candidate’s qualifications, complete curriculum vitae, and contact information for three letters of recommendation. Review of applications will begin October 31, 2026. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 31, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Appointment to begin August 1, 2027. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193863": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Assistant Teaching Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Psychology at Southern Methodist University seeks a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor to begin on August 1, 2027. This is a non-tenure track professional and teaching (P&T) faculty position with an initial contract for up to three years, renewable. Typical teaching load is twelve credit hours per semester (a 4/4 load) with opportunities for summer and intersession teaching. The Department of Psychology is part of Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences  (https://www.smu.edu/dedman) at SMU (https://www.smu.edu/). The department houses a PhD program in psychological clinical science, an MS program in organizational psychology, and undergraduate programs with one of the largest number of majors at the university. The department is comprised of outstanding faculty who collaborate to create a stimulating environment emphasizing excellence in both research and teaching. For additional information about the department, visit https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/psychology. SMU is a nationally-ranked, private, non-sectarian research university located in the heart of Dallas. SMU offers an intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, and business. SMU provides excellent benefits including full domestic partner benefits. The City of Dallas is one of the nation’s most cosmopolitan commercial and cultural centers. The Dallas–Fort Worth metroplex is also one of the nation’s fastest growing metropolitan areas and currently the fourth largest in the US. We are seeking candidates with teaching experience and the expertise to teach a breadth of courses in one or more of the following areas of psychology: introductory, developmental, social, personality, cognitive, organizational, and psychopathology. A PhD in psychology is required at the time of appointment. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193863. Interested applicants should submit: Cover letter; Curriculum vitae; Statement of teaching philosophy and teaching interests; Three letters of reference; and Evidence of teaching effectiveness. To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by January 15, 2027; however, the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. For questions about the search submission process or position, contact Dr. Holly Bowen, Assistant Teaching Professor Search Committee Chair at hbowen@smu.edu. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193864": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Lecturer in Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas Applications are invited for a full-time, non-tenure-track Lecturer in the Department of Mathematics position beginning August 1, 2027. The teaching load is eight courses per year, distributed across the Fall and Spring semesters. The Department of Mathematics is the academic home for research and teaching in mathematics at Southern Methodist University (SMU). In addition to our traditional undergraduate programs in applied and pure mathematics, the department participates in programs in data science and has launched a new major in mathematical economics. Additional information is available at https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/math. SMU is a nationally ranked private research university in Dallas, Texas offering a collegial academic environment, a broad interdisciplinary community, and access to the professional, cultural, and technological resources of the Dallas-Fort Worth metropolitan area. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. Applicants who hold a Ph.D. in mathematics or a related field by the time of appointment are preferred, although exceptional candidates with an M.S. degree will also be considered. Candidates should expect to teach primarily entry-level mathematics courses, such as Precalculus, Calculus I, and Business Calculus. The successful candidate must demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching and education and be willing to contribute to curricular development and mentorship. Applications and supporting documentation must be submitted online at https://apply.interfolio.com/193864. Interested applicants should submit: A cover letter, A curriculum vitae, At least two letters of recommendation, at least one of which must specifically address teaching, Teaching statement, and Evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as teaching evaluations or instructional materials. Candidates are also encouraged to submit supporting materials demonstrating successful teaching or pedagogical skills, such as instructional materials, or sample student evaluations. Applications received by January 1, 2027, will receive full consideration, although later applications may be considered until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its decision after the position has been filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193867": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Assistant Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas The Department of Mathematics at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a tenure track assistant professorship starting August 1, 2027. Preference will be given to candidates whose research program involves optimization, quantum computing, and/or machine learning for scientific computation. The department has nineteen tenure-stream faculty with active research efforts in numerical analysis, scientific computing, machine learning, data science, imaging, biology, nonlinear optics, and fluid mechanics. https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/math. Many additional opportunities for collaboration within the university with faculty members in statistics and data science, operations research, economics, electrical and computer engineering and other fields exist, and joint appointments are possible. Our Ph.D. program in Computational and Applied Mathematics is currently being modernized in light of the rapid changes in our field associated with the development of artificial intelligence and its applications in science and engineering, and the successful candidate will ideally contribute to this process. Besides our traditional undergraduate programs in applied and pure mathematics, the department participates in programs in data science and has launched a new major in mathematical economics. SMU has extensive high-performance computing facilities, which are freely available to all faculty, and a dedicated center for data science and research computing. https://www.smu.edu/provost/odonnell-institute. SMU seeks bold, curious and creative faculty to position our students for success. We offer the rare confluence of a comprehensive global research institution and a close-knit intellectual enterprise in the liberal arts tradition. Like our hometown of Dallas, our campus is vibrant. Here, innovative thinkers conduct research that has lasting impact and transform big ideas into new products, inventive technology and world-changing solutions. Our welcoming learning community of faculty and nearly 12,000 students in eight schools embraces a world of people and ideas. Come to SMU to think big and do good. Explore SMU at http://www.smu.edu. Candidates must have a PhD in applied mathematics, mathematics, or a closely related field. Postdoctoral experience is preferred. A cover letter, CV, research statement, teaching statement (including a description of teaching experience), and three letters of recommendation should be submitted to Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193867. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 30, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194569": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Lecturer in Medical Anthropology",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences Location: Dallas TX Join one of the oldest and most respected medical anthropology programs in the United States, at a university on the rise. The Department of Anthropology at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a Lecturer in Medical Anthropology to begin January 1, 2027. We are seeking an educator with a proven record of excellence to lead our existing introductory and upper-division courses in the field, including Health, Healing and Ethics and Health in Cross-Cultural Perspective. The Role This is a one-year, lecturer-track teaching appointment with a 4:4 load and no contractual research or service obligations, renewable for a second year pending satisfactory performance reviews. Responsibilities center on undergraduate teaching in medical anthropology, with occasional graduate-level teaching opportunities in the candidate’s area of expertise. Teaching faculty may apply for internal conference funds and competitive internal research funding. Why This Role Stands Out A department with deep roots in medical anthropology and faculty who currently hold competitive federal research grants from the National Institutes of Health and the National Science Foundation, among other funders An R-1 university with major momentum: a new $100 million Moody Graduate School, a new $30 million Peter O’Donnell Data Science Institute, and new membership in the Atlantic Coast Conference A position focused squarely on teaching Dallas—a cosmopolitan city with a relatively affordable cost of living, no state income tax, two major airports, a lively restaurant scene, professional sports, and a vibrant cultural life, in one of the fastest-growing large metropolitan areas in the country About the Department and SMU Anthropology has long been one of the strongest programs at SMU, and the department houses one of the oldest graduate medical anthropology programs in the United States. Our faculty advance intellectual frontiers, both ancient and modern, through interdisciplinary research, innovative teaching, and community outreach on global health and development, migration and borderland communities, and human impacts on and responses to environmental and health challenges. Our undergraduate, master’s, and Ph.D. programs help students build a complex understanding of differences, critical thinking, and global reach that prepares them for careers in academia and industry. Southern Methodist University is a secular, R-1 university with an intellectually vibrant community of internationally recognized scholars across the humanities, social sciences, mathematical sciences, engineering, law, fine arts, and business. SMU offers excellent benefits. Explore the Department of Anthropology at https://www.smu.edu/dedman/academics/departments/anthropology. Dedman College at https://www.smu.edu/dedman , and SMU at http://www.smu.edu. Minimum Qualifications Ph.D. in Anthropology or a related field in hand at the time of application Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate teaching in medical anthropology, including the ability to teach both introductory and upper-division courses Preferred Qualifications Experience teaching at the graduate level A teaching portfolio that reflects breadth across the field of medical anthropology Applications must be submitted via Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/194569 and should include: A cover letter addressing your teaching experience in medical anthropology A curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three references A short teaching portfolio containing at least two medical anthropology syllabi (one introductory, one upper division) and teaching evaluations from previously taught courses To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 29, 2026, but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to interview and give a teaching demonstration via Zoom. Questions may be directed to the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Eric G. Bing, at ebing@smu.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49201": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor of Accounting",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business Location: Dallas, TX Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Cox School of Business of Southern Methodist University in Dallas Texas. Per our faculty selection process, we typically set course schedules and make course staffing decisions a year in advance.  Thus for the current and next year, most course staffing decisions have already been completed. Please review the Cox School course offerings http://www.smu.edu/catalogs and craft a short memo which indicates your teaching interest, what content you feel most comfortable with, specifically if you have past undergraduate and graduate teaching experiences, and it will be in this file for the department chairs to review. At this time we are not delivering  course material exclusively on-line. Teaching for the department requires a Ph.D. in Accounting or a  related field or 10+ year of industry experience Upload your resume, a list of classes previously taught and memo indicating your teaching interests for review."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49203": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor of Information Technology & Operations Management",
      "description": "Posted: Sep 18, 2026 Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business Location: Dallas, TX Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Cox School of Business of Southern Methodist University in Dallas Texas. Per our faculty selection process, we typically set course schedules and make course staffing decisions a year in advance.  Thus for the current and next year, most course staffing decisions have already been completed. Please review the Cox School course offerings http://www.smu.edu/catalogs and craft a short memo which indicates your teaching interest, what content you feel most comfortable with, specifically if you have past undergraduate and graduate teaching experiences, and it will be in this file for the department chairs to review. At this time we are not delivering  course material exclusively on-line. Teaching for the department requires a Ph.D.  in a  related field or 10+ year of industry experience Upload your resume, a list of classes previously taught and memo indicating your teaching interests for review."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49204": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor of Marketing",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business Location: Dallas, TX Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Cox School of Business of Southern Methodist University in Dallas Texas. Per our faculty selection process, we typically set course schedules and make course staffing decisions a year in advance.  Thus for the current and next year, most course staffing decisions have already been completed. Please review the Cox School course offerings http://www.smu.edu/catalogs and craft a short memo which indicates your teaching interest, what content you feel most comfortable with, specifically if you have past undergraduate and graduate teaching experiences, and it will be in this file for the department chairs to review. At this time we are not delivering  course material exclusively on-line. Teaching for the department requires a Ph.D. in Marketing or a  related field or 10+ year of industry experience. Upload your resume, a list of classes previously taught and memo indicating your teaching interests for review."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49205": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor of Real Estate",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business Location: Dallas, TX Position Overview The SMU Cox School of Business is seeking an Adjunct Lecturer to teach a graduate-level Real Estate Development course beginning in March of each year.  This course is currently a 7-week course that immerses graduate students in the fundamentals of the real estate development process.  Historically, this course has been taught in a case study format using a local site.  Student teams will work together over the term of the course to take the tract from idea inception and feasibility, through the creation of a project concept and development program.  At the end of the course, student teams present their development plans to a juried panel of real estate industry executives. Desired Skills & Experience 10 years’ experience working as a real estate developer within a highly regarded commercial real estate organization MBA, M.S. in Real Estate Development or other relevant advanced degree required A strong understanding of macro and micro economic trends and of real estate development A strong understanding of market analysis, site entitlements, urban planning and design, financial analysis, and asset management Excellent writing, analytical and presentation skills and the ability to distill thoughts and ideas into lectures that resonate with students Excellent time management and organizational skills Gravitas and the ability to credibly represent SMU in a compelling way A broad network in the commercial real estate industry is valuable About SMU Cox & the Folsom Institute for Real Estate Celebrating over 100 years of business education, the Cox School of Business at Southern Methodist University is committed to influencing the way the world conducts business via prolific research that provokes innovation, change and global thought leadership. SMU Cox offers a full range of business education programs including BBA, Full-Time MBA, Professional MBA (part-time), Executive MBA and Master’s degree programs, as well as Executive Education and is accredited by the AACSB. Consistently ranked among the world’s leading business schools, SMU Cox maintains an active alumni network in more than 80 countries. The Margaret and Robert Folsom Institute for Real Estate was established at SMU Cox in 1984 through a generous gift from former Dallas mayor and real estate developer Robert Folsom.  It has been the backbone of real estate activity at SMU, supporting research and the real estate academic programs at both the BBA and MBA levels.  Today, SMU Cox is committed to building upon its success by broadening the Institute’s presence in the real estate industry on a national basis.  The expanded real estate program focuses on three key dimensions: academics, academic and applied research, and industry outreach."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49206": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor of Business Law",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business Location: Dallas, TX Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Cox School of Business of Southern Methodist University in Dallas Texas. Per our faculty selection process, we typically set course schedules and make course staffing decisions a year in advance.  Thus for the current and next year, most course staffing decisions have already been completed. Please review the Cox School course offerings http://www.smu.edu/catalogs and craft a short memo which indicates your teaching interest, what content you feel most comfortable with, specifically if you have past undergraduate and graduate teaching experiences, and it will be in this file for the department chairs to review. At this time we are not delivering  course material exclusively on-line. Teaching for the department requires a J.D. or a Ph.D. in a related field or 10+ year of industry experience. Upload your resume, a list of classes previously taught and memo indicating your teaching interests for review."
    },
