    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192293": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 Full Professor and Endowed Department Chair of Operations Research and Engineering Management (OREM)",
      "description": "Posted: Oct 09, 2026 Department: Southern Methodist University: Lyle School of Engineering Location: Dallas Position Description The Lyle School of Engineering at Southern Methodist University (SMU) invites accomplished candidates for the position of Full Professor and Endowed Department Chair of Operations Research and Engineering Management (OREM) . The successful candidate for chair of the department will also hold an endowed professorship with the anticipated start date of August 1, 2027. The Lyle School of Engineering has embarked on an ambitious journey, successfully hiring numerous outstanding faculty members, including several endowed professors during the recent past. Our culture of faculty growth thrives on innovation, fostering a dynamic and collaborative environment. We are committed to cutting-edge research funded by competitive federal grants, industry-funded projects and philanthropic gifts, which advances our academic goals and provides students with a competitive edge in the industry and/or academia. Our mission is to lead in the digital transformation of engineering education, equipping students with cutting-edge knowledge and skills to emerge as confident, capable leaders. Through excellence and innovation, we aim to significantly impact the engineering landscape and beyond. The OREM department at SMU has experienced significant growth in faculty research in the last several years. The department offers undergraduate degrees in Management Science (including the dual BS degree in Data Science-Management Science) and highly sought-after graduate degrees in Engineering Management (MS and DEng), Operations Research (MS and PhD), and Systems Engineering (MS). OREM’s graduate degree programs attract significant interest from industry professionals in the DFW metroplex. Historically, 65 to 75% of the student body is made up of graduate students. Coupled with the increasing externally funded research, the department is well poised to continue making a deep and lasting impact on the broader field of Industrial and Systems Engineering (ISE) and Operations Research (OR). Our faculty are actively engaged in research programs that cover a wide spectrum of theoretical and applied areas, including Optimization Methods, Applied Probability, Stochastics, Machine Learning, the Interface of Operations Research and Data Science, Infrastructure and Engineered Systems, and Supply Chain and Service Systems. This expertise forms a strong foundation, with a clear trajectory for expanding the department’s impact across all areas of ISE that align with our mission and degree programs. We are seeking a visionary leader to further elevate the department’s intellectual, creative, and scholarly development. The successful candidate will hold an earned doctoral degree and have relevant academic experience, coupled with international recognition in their discipline(s).  They will possess demonstrated excellence in technical, academic, and professional achievements, with a proven capacity for leadership. This is a unique opportunity for a dynamic leader who can inspire continued growth in cutting-edge research and innovation within Industrial and Systems Engineering, with a strong focus on expanding collaborations with federal agencies, industry, and other external partners. More information about the department is available at https://www.smu.edu/Lyle/Academics/Departments/OREM About the Bobby B. Lyle School of Engineering For nearly a century, Lyle has been nurturing exceptional engineers who are catalysts for global change. Our commitment to shaping bold leaders who innovate, pioneer, and tackle society’s toughest challenges is unwavering. Embedded in our mindset is a culture of ingenuity that resonates through our research and curriculum. With an impressive 9:1 graduate student-to-faculty ratio, coupled with robust scholarship offerings, we are dedicated to fostering meaningful mentorship and breaking engineering stereotypes. Our focus extends beyond campus, addressing critical global issues through groundbreaking research. Our five departments—Civil and Environmental Engineering, Computer Science, Electrical and Computer Engineering, Operations Research and Engineering Management, and Mechanical Engineering—drive impactful innovations beyond academia’s boundaries. Lyle School’s acclaimed centers and institutes include the Darwin Deason Institute for Cyber Security, Hart Center for Engineering Leadership, Caruth Institute for Engineering Education, Hunt Institute for Engineering and Humanity, Hart Institute for Technology, Innovation and Entrepreneurship, and the newly established Center for Digital and Human-Augmented Manufacturing. Our world-class research facilities are complemented by robust resources in high-performance scientific computing (see https://www.smu.edu/Provost/Data-Science-Institute/HPC ). About SMU Located near the center of Dallas, SMU is a private university of 12,000 students and offers strong undergraduate, graduate and professional programs through eight schools – Dedman College of Humanities and Sciences, Cox School of Business, Meadows School of the Arts, Lyle School of Engineering, Perkins School of Theology, Dedman School of Law, Simmons School of Education and Human Development, and the Moody Graduate School of Advanced Studies. To learn more about the rich cultural environment of SMU, please see: www.smu.edu. All applications should be submitted through Interfolio using the link below: https://apply.interfolio.com/192293 Equal Employment Opportunity Statement SMU is an equal opportunity employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity and expression. Qualifications for this position is an accomplished academic suitable for an endowed full professorship with an earned Ph.D. in the appropriate or a closely related discipline, with experience commensurate with the rank of full professor. The expertise will inspire and guide the next generation of trailblazers, bridging the gap between theory and real-world impact. Candidates are required to submit with their application a cover letter that includes research and teaching statements, a statement of leadership background and philosophy, Curriculum Vitae, and contact information for three professional references.  Successful candidates will also present a vision for continued growth and development of the department.  To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 20, 2026. The committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled and will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. The start date for this position is negotiable, but the desired start date is August 1, 2027. All applications should be submitted through Interfolio using the link below: https://apply.interfolio.com/192293"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191542": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #06367 Assistant Professor of Art History",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Department of Art History in the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University seeks a scholar and teacher of art and/or visual culture in the long nineteenth century with research/teaching specialization in any geographic/global region. The successful candidate will teach introductory and upper-level undergraduate lecture courses on nineteenth-century topics, as well as lecture courses and undergraduate and graduate seminars in their area of research specialization. This is a tenure-track position with a standard 2-2 course load, and includes advising graduate research at the MA and PhD levels. All faculty in the Department of Art History receive regular research leaves and research/publication support. We particularly encourage applications from candidates whose work crosses disciplinary boundaries; demonstrates theoretical/methodological fluency; engages with emerging scholarly fields in the humanities; and leverages/enhances the Department's existing strengths.  Our program prioritizes new approaches to historical and emerging media; transformative engagement with race and gender; analysis of architecture and the politics of space; and attention to global circuits of art and power. In collaboration with colleagues in allied specialties in the Department of Art History, the candidate will assume an important role in shaping the future of research, curriculum, and pedagogy at our institution.  The candidate will develop courses for undergraduate and graduate students, both general surveys and focused research seminars; mentor students on all levels; supervise MA and PhD theses; and contribute to the overall vitality of the University through participation in interdisciplinary programs within the SMU Meadows School of the Arts and across campus. Position Start Date: 1 August 2027 . Minimum Qualifications ·      Ph.D. in Art History or related field in-hand by August 2027 Applications must include: ·      Cover Letter, CV, one or more publications/writing sample. ·      Names/contact information of three professional references. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191542. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by November 20, 2026 , though the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. About the Meadows School of the Arts Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non- Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry. research. About SMU Southern Methodist University , a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students, is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. SMU sits in the heart of the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, a culturally rich global arts, business and tech hub and home to over eight million people, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191543": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #50232 Assistant or Associate Professor of Theatre Studies",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Division of Theatre in the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor of Theatre , with a specialization in Theatre Studies and/or Playwriting , beginning August 1, 2027 . We seek an exceptional scholar-practitioner whose work reflects sustained engagement with both the practice and study of theatre. The successful candidate will contribute to the intellectual and creative life of the Division through the teaching of theatre studies courses, including playwriting, while maintaining an active scholarly and/or creative practice. We welcome applicants whose work bridges disciplinary boundaries and who value collaboration, intellectual rigor, and the transformative possibilities of theatre. This appointment will play an important role in the continued growth of the Division. Alongside our nationally recognized BFA and MFA conservatory programs, the recent launch of the Sexton Institute for Musical Theatre has created new opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration while reinforcing our commitment to rigorous artistic training, intellectual curiosity, and individualized mentorship. Responsibilities The successful candidate will contribute to the undergraduate theatre studies curriculum through the teaching of courses that may include playwriting, theatre history, dramaturgy, text analysis, performance studies, and other offerings aligned with the candidate's expertise and the needs of the Division. Opportunities for graduate teaching, particularly within the MFA program, may also be available. Additional responsibilities include: •  Maintaining an active scholarly, creative, and/or professional practice. • Mentoring undergraduate students. • Participating in curriculum development and assessment. • Engaging in divisional, school, and university service. • Collaborating with colleagues across disciplines in support of productions, new work development, and other artistic initiatives. Required Qualifications • Terminal degree (Ph.D., MFA, or equivalent professional distinction appropriate to the appointment). • An active record of scholarly, creative, and/or professional achievement. • Demonstrated ability to teach undergraduate theatre studies courses that complement both the needs of the Division and the candidate's areas of expertise. • Evidence of collaborative engagement and a commitment to mentoring emerging theatre artists and scholars. Application Materials Applicants should submit: • Letter of interest. • Curriculum Vitae. • Statement of teaching philosophy • A writing sample of no more than 25 pages (can include a sample from a play or scholarly writing) • Contact information for three professional references. Additional materials may be requested as the search progresses. Review of applications will begin on October 30 th , 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191543. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 30 , 2026, though the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. About the Division of Theatre The Division of Theatre offers nationally recognized BFA and MFA degree programs that combine conservatory-style training with the intellectual breadth of a leading research university. As a praxis-based program, we believe theatre is simultaneously an artistic, intellectual, and civic practice. Through intensive studio training, rigorous academic inquiry, and sustained creative practice, students develop as thoughtful artists, skilled collaborators, and engaged citizens. Our faculty are accomplished artists, educators, and scholars whose active professional careers enrich the classroom, studio, and rehearsal hall while fostering curiosity, collaboration, and artistic excellence. Situated in one of the nation's most vibrant performing arts communities, the Division benefits from Dallas's remarkable professional theatre ecosystem. A longstanding relationship with the Tony Award-winning Dallas Theater Center, together with an extraordinary community of regional theatres, independent artists, and producers of new work, creates exceptional opportunities for artistic collaboration, professional engagement, and creative research. About the Meadows School of the Arts Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non- Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation, and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry. research. About SMU Southern Methodist University , a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students, is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. SMU sits in the heart of the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, a culturally rich global arts, business and tech hub and home to over eight million people, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191544": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #50898 Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice in CCPA - Public Relations and Strategic Communication",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas SMU’s Division of Corporate Communication and Public Affairs is searching for a Public Relations and Strategic Communication Professor. More specifically, we're looking for someone who has actually done the work, built the strategy, taken the 2 a.m. call, and knows the difference between a good pitch and a great one. Our CCPA faculty are award-winning practitioners, researchers, and communicators who show up for students the way great mentors showed up for them. If that sounds like you, we want to talk. What You'll Bring You've spent real years in the field, agency, corporate, in-house, nonprofit, wherever the work happens. You've built PR strategies that moved the needle on reputation and visibility. You know media relations from the inside: pitching, placement, and what to do when a story you didn't want breaks anyway. You've led crisis response when the stakes were real. And now you're ready to translate that instinct into something teachable. What You'll Do Teach a full course load across our undergraduate PR and strategic communication curriculum. It includes courses like Newswriting for PR , Principles of Public Relations , Crisis Management , Principles of Digital Communication & Social Media , plus opportunities to shape special topics courses around your own expertise. Mentor students who are hungry to learn both what is in the book and the things that a textbook can't teach them. Bring your professional network into the classroom, whether that's guest speakers, internship pipelines, or simply war stories that make the theory click. This is a Professional and Teaching faculty position. The typical teaching load for this position is a 4/4. While it is not eligible for tenure, it has a three-tier promotion path. Professional and Teaching faculty’s primary responsibility is teaching. Creative work and research are not expected, though they may be rewarded. Required Qualifications Meaningful, hands-on professional experience in public relations or a closely related field Demonstrated potential for excellence in undergraduate teaching Ability to teach across a range of PR courses, from foundational writing to strategy and ethics Strong interpersonal and written communication skills across a culturally diverse student and colleague community B.A. in Communications, PR, Journalism, or related discipline. Preferred Qualifications Experience integrating digital strategy, social analytics, or emerging communications technology into teaching or practice Advanced degree in Communications, PR, Journalism, or related discipline PRSA membership, APR accreditation, or equivalent professional credentialing A track record of mentoring early-career professionals or students An active professional network you're excited to open up to students Applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio: ·      A formal letter of application detailing how your experience overlaps with the divisions’ strengths and interest in teaching; ·      A C.V. or professional resume; ·      A list of three references with contact information (references will not be contacted without the applicant's knowledge). For priority consideration, applications should be submitted by 11:59 p.m. CT on October 31, 2026. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191544 . Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Questions may be directed to the search chair, Stacia Philips at sphilips@smu.edu . Why SMU, Why Dallas? Dallas is one of the most dynamic media and corporate communications markets in the country, and our students benefit from being embedded in it. You'll join a department that's actively investing in its future, with a faculty culture built on genuinely liking each other, not just tolerating each other. SMU, a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students , is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. The Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex is a culturally rich arts and global business center, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy. The Division of Corporate Communication and Public Affairs , housed within Meadows School of the Arts, is home to nearly 200 majors across degree specializations in Organizational Communication, Public Relations & Strategic Communication and Public Affairs & Political Communication."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191547": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice, Fashion Media",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Division of Journalism seeks candidates for a professor of practice position to teach in the division's popular Fashion Media program beginning August 2027. The successful candidate will join the division at an exciting period of expansion and innovation, and the successful candidate must be an engaged teacher and mentor with significant industry experience in storytelling across current and emerging platforms. Specifically, we seek candidates with a backing in social media content production, ideally with experience concepting and executing multi-media fashion-related content and promotion. Ideally, individuals will have worked for fashion and lifestyle outlets or brands and be familiar with audience engagement techniques. Depending on their background, we anticipate the new hire might teach classes like Fashion & Digital Culture, Fashion Promotion and Fashion Journalism and will develop the social media production courses and lead content creation labs. Previous teaching experience is preferred but not required. We are especially interested in candidates who bring a collaborative spirit, a commitment to ethical and inclusive storytelling across creative media for current and emerging platforms (including social media and experiential events), a willingness to share their professional networks as part of their work with students, and the ability to develop internships and media partnerships. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in a fashion-, communication- or journalism-related field required; a Master’s degree is preferred but not required. Five years producing storytelling content within the fashion and lifestyle related realms Content-creation experience with a national or international brand Ability to teach classes both hands-on and theoretical courses involving fashion promotion and social media content creation. We're especially interested in hearing from applicants who have: Prior collegiate-level teaching experience Significant fashion or lifestyle brand experience A passion for both emerging technologies like AI and traditional media like magazines Applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191547 A formal letter of application addressing teaching philosophy and interest in this position A C.V. A professional portfolio or website link showing past work including campaigns A list of three references with contact information (references will not be contacted without the applicant's knowledge). To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by Nov. 13, 2026 , but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of the employment decision after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. Questions may be directed to the search chair, Jenny Davis, at jennydavis@smu.edu . SMU is a private university dedicated to academic excellence. Located in Dallas, SMU maintains a moderate size of 12,000 students. SMU is designated as a preferred employer in the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, one of the most prolific high-tech industrial centers in the country. The Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex is a multi-faceted business and engineering community, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy. Dallas’ quality of life is exceptional with a relatively low cost of living, upscale apartments and homes within walking distance of campus, the opportunity to live in the city or out in the country with a relatively short commute, and the availability of both mass transit systems and plentiful on-campus parking. Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts and communication education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non-Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry research. The Division of Journalism, housed within Meadows School of the Arts, is a forward-thinking community of scholars and professionals that offers degrees in Fashion Media and Journalism, along with business and sports journalism concentrations. Visit us at https://www.smu.edu/meadows/areasofstudy/journalism/undergraduatestudies/fashionmediaba"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191548": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice and Director of Sports Media",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Division of Journalism invites applications for a non-tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor of Practice who will serve as Director of Sports Media , teaching courses for the Division’s Sports Journalism Specialization and providing leadership in expanding the program. The anticipated teaching workload is three courses per semester, including the Division’s existing sports-focused courses and other courses depending on area of expertise. Additional responsibilities also will include updating and shaping curriculum, committee work, student media advising and/or developing internships, guest lectures, and other opportunities for students. The sports media director will join a growing, innovative community that seeks to provide students with the skills, opportunities, and conceptual knowledge needed to succeed in the fast-changing media landscape. The successful candidate will collaborate with sports-focused programs across campus, leveraging the rising profile of SMU Athletics in the Atlantic Coast Conference as well as the DFW region’s reputation as an epicenter of sports media. The position begins August 2027. Minimum requirements for the position include: ● A bachelor's degree in media, journalism, communications, or related field; and ● At least five years of professional media experience, including substantial experience with sports coverage. Prior university teaching experience and a master's degree in a relevant field are preferred. The ideal candidate will possess at least three years of experience with a major-market, regional or national media organization, delivering content in multiple formats. Prior leadership experience also is preferred, as is a proficient understanding of sports analytics and experience training and mentoring students and/or early career journalists. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 26, 2026 , but the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. Applications should include 1) a cover letter describing relevant experience, teaching philosophy, and reason for interest in the position; 2) a curriculum vitae; and 3) the names and contact information for three references. Applications must be submitted online through Interfolio via the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191548 For questions about the position and application process, please contact search committee chair Jake Batsell at jbatsell@smu.edu . The Division of Journalism , housed within the Meadows School of the Arts, is home to more than 200 majors across degree programs in Journalism and Fashion Media, along with specializations in business journalism and sports journalism. Our student media outlets provide opportunities to create professional-level work and develop leadership abilities, and small class sizes allow faculty to know their students and foster personalized instruction and mentorship. Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts and communication education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non-Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry research. SMU , a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students, is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. SMU sits in the heart of the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, a culturally rich global arts, business and tech hub and home to over eight million people, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191550": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #06345 Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice in Film & Media Arts - Production",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Division of Film & Media Arts in the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is seeking a top-notch production teacher to join a creative and productive faculty and student body located in one of the leading media markets in the nation. The ideal candidate is one who can effectively mentor student filmmakers at all levels, helping them identify and utilize their own creative voices through a variety of media forms. Teaching will cover introductory, intermediate, and advanced undergraduate courses in film and media production, as well as specialized courses in the candidate's particular area(s) of expertise and interest. Minimum qualifications: MFA or equivalent is required, along with previous teaching experience. Must have strong and broad skills in the technical aspects of filmmaking, with specialized expertise in at least one “craft” area (e.g., cinematography, sound, editing, etc.). Preferred qualifications: Experience in short-form or long-form narrative production and/or production for social media. This is a renewable, professional (i.e., non-tenure) track position with a start date in August 2027. Application: Please submit a vita, and a statement of teaching philosophy and experience. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191550 To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 17, 2026 . The committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. The committee will notify applicants of its employment decisions after the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. About the Division of Film & Media Arts The Division of Film & Media Arts is home to approximately 100 undergraduate majors in its Bachelor of Arts and Bachelor of Fine Arts programs, with the latter offering a choice of concentrations between production and screenwriting. Our program merges practical and conceptual aspects of media and encourages interdisciplinary connections to related fields. Our faculty includes a uniquely talented group of writers, scholars, and award- winning film and video makers. Alumni have become successful industry professionals with distinctions including festival recognition, Emmys, Academy Awards, and many other honors. Visit the Meadows School and the Division’s web site for detailed information http://smu.edu/meadows/ About the Meadows School of the Arts Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non- Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry. research. About SMU Southern Methodist University , a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students, is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. SMU sits in the heart of the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, a culturally rich global arts, business and tech hub and home to over eight million people, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191551": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #06292 Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice in Costume Design",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Meadows School of the Arts Location: Dallas The Division of Theatre in the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist Universityn invites applications for a full-time, professional-track appointment at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor of Practice in Costume Design , beginning August 1, 2027 . We seek an accomplished costume designer whose professional practice reflects artistic excellence, collaborative engagement, and a commitment to mentoring emerging designers. The successful candidate will foster the development of the MFA costume design students in an unusually rich production environment spanning theatre, opera, dance, and musical theatre. We welcome applicants whose professional life actively informs their teaching and who is passionate about helping students develop both technical mastery and distinctive artistic voices. This position offers exceptional opportunities to collaborate across disciplines while preparing graduate designers for successful professional careers. Alongside our nationally recognized BFA and MFA conservatory programs, the Division recently launched the Sexton Institute for Musical Theatre and continues to expand opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration. This integrated production environment is central to the Division's educational mission and supports the continued growth of the MFA program and the expanding Musical Theatre initiative. This is a renewable, professional (i.e., non-tenure) track position with a start date in August 2027. Responsibilities The successful candidate will play a central role in the education and mentorship of MFA costume designers and the artistic life of the Division. Responsibilities include: • Teaching graduate courses in costume design, design history, and related skills. • Mentoring MFA costume designers through studio instruction, portfolio development, and realized productions. • Designing productions within the Division's production season. • Collaborating closely with faculty and production staff across Theatre, Music, Dance, and the Sexton Institute for Musical Theatre. • Supervising graduate assistants and working in partnership with the Costume Shop Director and production faculty. • Participating in MFA Design recruitment, curriculum development, and assessment. • Engaging in divisional, school, and university service. • Maintaining an active professional costume design practice. Required Qualifications • MFA in Costume Design or equivalent professional distinction. • Significant professional experience in costume design for theatre, opera, dance, or related live performance. • Demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentorship. • Evidence of an active professional design practice. • Evidence of collaborative engagement and a commitment to mentoring emerging theatre artists and designers. • An understanding of contemporary design technologies and digital tools. Application Materials Applicants should submit: • Letter of interest. • Curriculum Vitae. • Statement of teaching philosophy. • Digital portfolio of professional design work. • Contact information for three professional references. Additional materials—including examples of student mentorship, realized productions, or syllabi—may be requested as the search progresses. Review of applications will begin on October 30 th , 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Applications must be submitted electronically via Interfolio at the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/191551 . To ensure full consideration for the position, the application must be received by October 30, 2026 , though the committee will continue to accept applications until the position is filled. Hiring is contingent upon the satisfactory completion of a background check. About the Division of Theatre The Division of Theatre offers nationally recognized BFA and MFA degree programs that combine conservatory-style training with the intellectual breadth of a leading research university. As a praxis-based program, we believe theatre is simultaneously an artistic, intellectual, and civic practice. Through intensive studio training, rigorous academic inquiry, and sustained creative practice, students develop as thoughtful artists, skilled collaborators, and engaged citizens. Our faculty are accomplished artists, educators, and scholars whose active professional careers enrich the classroom, studio, and rehearsal hall while fostering curiosity, collaboration, and artistic excellence. Situated in one of the nation's most vibrant performing arts communities, the Division benefits from Dallas's remarkable professional theatre ecosystem. A longstanding relationship with the Tony Award-winning Dallas Theater Center, together with an extraordinary community of regional theatres, independent artists, and producers of new work, creates exceptional opportunities for artistic collaboration, professional engagement, and creative research. About the Meadows School of the Arts Founded in 1969, the Meadows School of the Arts at Southern Methodist University is one of the foremost arts education institutions in the United States. The Meadows School offers undergraduate and graduate degrees in Advertising, Art, Art History, Arts and Non- Profit Leadership, Corporate Communication and Public Affairs, Creative Computation, Dance, Design and Innovation, Film and Media Arts, Game Development, Journalism, Music, and Theatre. The Meadows School is a leader in curricular innovation and challenges its students to make a difference locally and globally through building vital connections between the arts and society. The Meadows School of the Arts is also a convener for the arts in North Texas, serving as a catalyst for new collaborations and providing critical industry. research. About SMU Southern Methodist University , a private nonsectarian university of roughly 12,000 students, is an inclusive and intellectually diverse community of teachers and scholars that values diverse research and creative agendas and is dedicated to academic excellence. SMU sits in the heart of the Dallas/Fort Worth metroplex, a culturally rich global arts, business and tech hub and home to over eight million people, offering exceptional museums, diverse cultural attractions and a vibrant economy."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190898": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 - #52843 Professor of Tax Law",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman School of Law Location: Dallas SMU DEDMAN SCHOOL OF LAW is seeking applications from qualified persons for tenured and tenure-track faculty positions to begin Fall of 2027. We seek candidates with expertise in the following areas: Criminal Law, Evidence, Tax Law, and Oil & Gas/Natural Resources/Energy Law. We will consider entry-level applicants with excellent credentials and scholarly distinction or promise for criminal law and evidence. We seek established tenured scholars with strong scholarly records for tax law and oil & gas/natural resources/energy law. Candidates must hold a Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree.  To ensure full consideration for the positions, applications must be received by September 15, 2026 , but the committee will continue to accept applications until the positions are filled. Candidates must hold a Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree.  To ensure full consideration for the positions, applications must be received by September 15, 2026 , but the committee will continue to accept applications until the positions are filled. Applications should be submitted in Interfolio, even if the candidate has submitted an application to the AALS Faculty Appointments Register. Prospective candidates identified by the committee in the FAR will be requested to also submit their materials through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190899": {
      "title": "SMU 26-27 #5760 George W. Hutchison Chair of Energy Law and Professor of Law",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Dedman School of Law Location: Dallas SMU DEDMAN SCHOOL OF LAW is seeking applications from qualified persons for tenured and tenure-track faculty positions to begin Fall of 2027. We seek candidates with expertise in the following areas: Criminal Law, Evidence, Tax Law, and Oil & Gas/Natural Resources/Energy Law. We will consider entry-level applicants with excellent credentials and scholarly distinction or promise for criminal law and evidence. We seek established tenured scholars with strong scholarly records for tax law and oil & gas/natural resources/energy law. Candidates must hold a Juris Doctor (J.D.) degree. To ensure full consideration for the positions, applications must be received by September 15, 2026 , but the committee will continue to accept applications until the positions are filled. Applications should be submitted in Interfolio, even if the candidate has submitted an application to the AALS Faculty Appointments Register. Prospective candidates identified by the committee in the FAR will be requested to also submit their materials through Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/49131": {
      "title": "SMU Cox Adjunct Professor in Finance",
      "description": "Department: Southern Methodist University: Cox School of Business: Department of Finance Location: Dallas, TX Thank you for your interest in teaching at the Cox School of Business of Southern Methodist University in Dallas Texas. Per our faculty selection process, we typically set course schedules and make course staffing decisions a year in advance.  Thus, for the current and next year, most course staffing decisions have already been completed. However, we sometimes have needs that arise during the year. Please review the Cox School course offerings http://www.smu.edu/catalogs and craft a short memo which indicates your teaching interest, what content you feel most comfortable with, specifically if you have past undergraduate and graduate teaching experiences, and it will be in this file for the department chairs to review. At this time we are not delivering  course material exclusively on-line. Teaching for the Finance department generally requires a Ph.D. in Finance or a finance related field and 10+ year of industry experience. Upload your resume, a list of classes previously taught and memo indicating your teaching interests for review."
    }
  },
